## Labels

Rule-derived from action bands, not from observed plant health:

| Variable | Band |
|---|---|
| pH | 5.5 – 7.0 |
| EC | 1.2 – 2.5 mS/cm |
| Air temperature | 18 – 30 °C |
| Humidity | 50 – 80 % |
| Water temperature | 18 – 26 °C |

Inside every band → `Normal`. One variable out → that variable's class
(`EC_High`, `pH_Low`, `WaterTemp_High`, …). Two or more → `Multiple_Stress`.

Bands are wider than textbook optima on purpose. With strict lettuce ranges
(pH 5.5–6.5, water temp 18–22 °C), 78 % of rows came out `Multiple_Stress`
because the crop was grown in a warm climate. Cite whichever ranges you adopt.

Full-dataset distribution: Normal 327, Multiple_Stress 135, Humidity_Low 87,
WaterTemp_High 75, EC_High 58, pH_High 25, pH_Low 10, WaterTemp_Low 8,
EC_Low 1. The last two classes are dropped from the splits — 8 and 1 rows are
too few to learn or to score.

## Splits

**Every 4th calendar day of each experiment is held out as test.** No day
appears on both sides, so a tank's own reading cannot sit in train and test at
once.

Two other splits were tried and rejected:
- **Random row split** — six tanks share the same day and air readings, so rows
  leak across the split and accuracy is meaningless.
- **Whole-experiment holdout** (train EXP.1+2, test EXP.3) — each experiment ran
  different conditions, so EXP.3 contains classes EXP.1 never produced. The test
  set ended up with only 3 of 7 classes.

**Balancing is applied to the training split only** (oversampling to 240 rows
per class). The test set keeps its real distribution. Balancing the whole
dataset before splitting would put copies of the same row on both sides.

## Measured performance

Final model: Random Forest (200 trees, `max_depth=8`, `min_samples_leaf=1`),
5 input features (`ph, ec, water_temp, humidity, air_temp`), target `label`.

| Evaluation | Result |
|---|---|
| Single held-out split (every 4th day, 162 rows) | accuracy 0.994, macro-F1 0.986 |
| 5-fold cross-validation grouped by date (all real rows) | macro-F1 **0.837 +/- 0.075** |

Report the grouped cross-validation figure as the expected performance. The
single-split score is one favourable split and should be labelled as such.

## What to say about these numbers

The label is a deterministic function of the features, so the model relearns the
thresholds that produced the labels (the printed decision tree splits on
pH 5.45 / 7.05, EC 2.50 and so on). High single-split accuracy reflects that, not
independently validated plant health.

Fold scores range from 0.756 to 0.951 because pH_Low (10 rows) and pH_High
(25 rows) are rare. Tuning, balanced oversampling, SMOTE (failed: too few real
rows per fold) and merging the two pH classes (no improvement) were all tried.

## Honest limitations for your report

- Labels come from published reference ranges, not from observed plant health.
  No agronomist confirmed that a row labelled `EC_High` had an unhealthy plant.
- 726 real readings is small. The balanced training file is 1680 rows, but those
  are oversampled copies, not new measurements.
- Only 726 rows have pH and EC. The 2069 hourly rows have no water chemistry.
- The image dataset (Kaggle Lettuce NPK) is a separate collection with no
  timestamp link to these sensors. The two models are combined by rules, not by
  learned fusion, and no such link is claimed.
- Crop is lettuce; the target deployment is tomato.
